        <tr><td><span id="F-49"></span>F-49</td><td>Irregularity (upstream demo data)</td><td><strong>The demo API returns prices that contradict each other.</strong> One sampled row for <code>KXPRESPERSON-28-GABB</code> came back with <code>last_price_dollars: "0.8030"</code> and <code>yes_ask_dollars: "0.8030"</code> while <code>no_bid_dollars</code> was <code>0.9970</code>. A binary book must satisfy yes ask + no bid = $1.00 (Orderbook Responses), so that row is impossible: taken at face value it is a fake $0.80 crash from $0.003. Reading the same market directly returned the healthy book yes 0.0020/0.0030, no 0.9970/0.9980, previous price 0.0030. A second case: <code>KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B47500</code> reported last price 0.98 against a 0.05/0.06 book. The alert engine therefore validates every row against reciprocity, rejects a stale last price on a tight book, and publishes a sampled alert only when the new price is corroborated by a second published field; anything else is suppressed and counted. Full method: <a href="docs/ALERTS.md">docs/ALERTS.md</a>.</td><td>Live alert engine; alert feeds</td><td><a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/KXPRESPERSON-28-GABB" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the market, read directly</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets?series_ticker=KXPRESPERSON&status=open&limit=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the list read that returned 0.8030</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/orderbook_responses" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Orderbook Responses (the $1 reciprocity)</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-50"></span>F-50</td><td>Irregularity (upstream)</td><td><code>/search/tags_by_categories</code> has no <code>Culture</code> key, yet <code>GET /series?category=Culture</code> returns series whose <code>category</code> is <code>Culture</code> (for example <code>KXXBANNEDGROK</code>). The alert engine therefore treats an unkeyed category as a recorded note and lets discovery prove it, while still failing the run when a tag is missing from a category that does publish tags.</td><td>Live alert engine; topic rules</td><td><a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/search/tags_by_categories" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tag taxonomy</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/series?category=Culture&include_volume=true&limit=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culture series</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-series-list" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get Series List</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-51"></span>F-51</td><td>Resolved (measured 2026-11-21)</td><td>Same question as F-01, asked about the console under <code>alerts/</code>. Measured on a GitHub runner: the demo API answers public reads with this origin echoed in <code>access-control-allow-origin</code>, so the console’s live mode is not blocked by the exchange. What remains true, and is unchanged by that measurement: (a) a visitor’s own network can still block the host, and the console reports that in its banner and falls back to the engine feed; (b) the WebSocket ticker channel needs API-key authentication during the handshake, so a static page cannot stream without holding a key — it is not used. See <a href="data/cors-probe.json">the raw headers</a> and <a href="verification/2026-11-21-c85ee474.md">the method</a>.</td><td>alerts/ console</td><td><a href="data/cors-probe.json">Measured CORS headers</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/quick_start_market_data" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quick Start: Market Data</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/quick_start_websockets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quick Start: WebSockets</a></td><td><span class="badge ok">Resolved</span></td></tr>
