        <tr><td><span id="F-60"></span>F-60</td><td>Repo irregularities (fixed 2026-11-21)</td><td>Line-by-line review (session-5bba56cd) found and fixed four additional defects: (a) <code>templates/{index,dashboard,markets,market,portfolio,analysis}.tpl</code> omitted <code>data-page="..."</code> on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>, preventing <code>assets/app.js</code> from running those page controllers (fixed in templates + pathname fallback in <code>assets/app.js</code>); (b) <code>assets/app.js</code> <code>pages.market</code> had no default ticker when opened without <code>?ticker=</code>, clobbered snapshot titles on API failure, omitted <code>#m-roi2</code>, and used <code>colspan="4"</code>/<code>"10"</code> on 5/11-column tables; (c) <code>tools/live_engine.mjs</code> and <code>alerts/js/alerts-live.js</code> still allowed 3&ndash;14 min cross-detector repeats on the same ticker (<code>KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500</code> 19/31 rows, failing <code>scripts/check_alerts.mjs</code>), fixed by enforcing one swing per ticker per 15 min across all detectors; (d) added duplicate HTML <code>id</code> check to <code>scripts/check-links.mjs</code>.</td><td>Whole site; alerts; QA</td><td><a href="verification/2026-11-21-5bba56cd.md">verification/2026-11-21-5bba56cd.md</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">live KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO (verified 2026-11-21)</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed in this session</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-62"></span>F-62</td><td>Verification limitation (undocumented query)</td><td><strong>Reclassified 2026-11-21:</strong> the catalyst page, its script, and the strategy-lab topic row describe <code>/events?category=</code> as an unreliable server-side category filter. The official <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/events/get-events.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get Events</a> OpenAPI parameter list documents <code>limit</code>, <code>cursor</code>, <code>with_nested_markets</code>, <code>with_milestones</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>series_ticker</code>, <code>tickers</code>, <code>min_close_ts</code>, and <code>min_updated_ts</code>; it does not list <code>category</code>. Therefore the previously recorded query using <code>category=Crypto</code> does not establish failure of a documented filter. Root flag F-01 is the browser-CORS limitation and does not substantiate this claim. <strong>Corrected 2026-11-21 (session-c144ad6a):</strong> the catalyst page/script and the strategy-lab topic row no longer describe an “unreliable” server-side category filter; the undocumented <code>category=</code> discovery queries were removed in favour of the documented <code>status=open&amp;with_nested_markets=true&amp;limit=200</code> query with client-side mapping; the F-01 cross-reference was corrected to F-62; an undocumented <code>limit</code> parameter on the catalyst <code>/series</code> call was removed. See <code>verification/2026-11-21-c144ad6a.md</code>.</td><td>Catalyst discovery; strategy topic label</td><td><a href="catalyst.html">Catalyst page (claim under review)</a> · <a href="strategylab.html">Strategy lab (cross-reference under review)</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/events?category=Crypto&amp;status=open&amp;limit=3&amp;with_nested_markets=true" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Earlier probe URL (undocumented parameter)</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/events/get-events.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official Get Events parameters</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-20-e6c3b3b6.md">Earlier K1 record</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Corrected 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-BFC-01"></span>F-BFC-01</td><td>Observation (feed vs narrative)</td><td>The committed feed held 99 alert rows at the 2026-11-21 audit (all one ticker, <code>KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO</code>, 2026-11-20 16:28:00Z → 19:25:54Z; largest move $0.80) and 173 rows after the 2026-11-21 06:28:52 sample (<code>KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO</code> 99 + <code>KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500</code> 74; latest run created 0 new trade alerts and added 60 candle alerts, 242 duplicates collapsed). This conflicts with <code>docs/ALERTS.md</code>'s "0 after the reset" phrasing and with "the feeds say so": the feed persists the retained detected window rather than resetting to empty. The historical $0.80 swing must not be acted on without review. PR #39 (one swing per ticker per 15 min) addresses the flood; it has since merged (the feed's committed counts are the honest read; no deduplication is applied at render time). <em>Reconciled 2026-11-21 by the final integration session: the prose in docs/ALERTS.md now states the current counts and points here.</em></td><td>Live alert feeds</td><td><a href="data/live/feed.json">data/live/feed.json</a> · <a href="docs/ALERTS.md">docs/ALERTS.md</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/pull/39">PR #39</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-bfc2de45.md">F-BFC record</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-BFC-03"></span>F-BFC-03</td><td>Observation (extreme demo-price oscillation)</td><td>The post-fix feed holds 74 <code>KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500</code> alerts between 03:29:00Z and 06:28:45Z, with repeated $0.96 moves between $0.01 and $0.97. The latest run added 60 candle alerts; across the full feed there are 173 rows, 159 trade detections, 14 candle detections, 18 highROI labels and 0 resolutions. <em>Correction, 2026-11-21:</em> those 18 labels came from the pre-fix model; the five rows listed in <code>meta.legacy_unfillable_high_roi</code> have since been re-scored below the threshold (see F-71), and the committed sample now carries 1 highROI label. The official market response reported last price $0.0100, previous price $0.9700, YES bid $0.0000 and YES ask $0.0100; official trades endpoints returned both $0.0100 and $0.9700 rows close together. These are observed demo API values — not a claim that the response is erroneous — but large oscillations with a wide book must not be treated as validated ROI or executable prices without review. See F-BFC-01 for the feed/narrative split.</td><td>Live alert feeds; model output</td><td><a href="data/live/feed.json">data/live/feed.json</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500">official BTC market</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/trades?ticker=KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500&amp;min_ts=1795231740&amp;limit=10">official BTC trades</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-bfc2de45.md">F-BFC record</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-63"></span>F-63</td><td>Defect (found in this review, fixed)</td><td><strong>A regression check that could never fail.</strong> The chat-readability guard added with PR #39 in <code>scripts/check_alerts.mjs</code> was placed <em>after</em> the <code>if (problems.length) { &hellip; process.exit(1) }</code> block. <code>fail()</code> only appends to that array, so the guard recorded a violation nobody read and the checker printed <code>OK:</code> with exit code 0 on the very feed it was written to reject (measured: 173 rows, 99 from one ticker, 30 rows from that ticker inside one rolling hour). <strong>Fixed</strong> by moving the guard before the exit check, replacing the &ldquo;more than half of all rows&rdquo; ratio with an enforceable cap of <strong>12 alerts per ticker per rolling hour</strong> &mdash; the engine’s own capacity, 3 detectors &times; 4 quarter-hour windows, and about half the 22&ndash;30/hour measured on the broken feed, downgrading the ratio to a warning, and adding <code>tools/check_alerts_guard_test.mjs</code> &mdash; which drives the real checker with a real flood and asserts exit code 1. A first threshold of 6/hour sat below the engine’s real capacity and failed a live CI sample at 2026-11-21 06:38 UTC; the cap was re-derived from the detector count rather than loosened by guesswork, and that failed run is recorded here instead of being hidden. Sources for the flood figures: the committed feed before collapsing.</td><td><code>scripts/check_alerts.mjs</code> · CI <code>alerts-qa</code></td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/scripts/check_alerts.mjs">check_alerts.mjs</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/tools/check_alerts_guard_test.mjs">guard test</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/pull/39">PR #39</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-64"></span>F-64</td><td>Process gap (closed)</td><td><strong>Duplicate HTML anchors kept reaching production because no check looked for them.</strong> The Flagged for Review page has carried a repeated id three times in this project’s history (F-27&hellip;F-31 in two different flag sets, then F-54 twice, one upstream-API finding and one environment finding). Each time it was found by hand after publication, and each duplicate silently breaks the in-page anchor links that other pages use to cite a flag. CI watched for merge-conflict markers and broken links but never for repeated ids. <strong>Closed:</strong> <code>scripts/check-html-ids.mjs</code> now scans every published HTML file (89 files, 542 ids at the time of writing) for repeated ids and runs in the <code>site-qa</code> workflow. Current result: no duplicate ids.</td><td>Whole published tree</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/scripts/check-html-ids.mjs">check-html-ids.mjs</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/actions/workflows/site-qa.yml">site-qa workflow</a> · <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#the-id-attribute">HTML spec: ids must be unique</a></td><td><span class="badge neutral">Documented</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-69"></span>F-69</td><td>Defect (found in this review, fixed)</td><td><strong>The market price chart did not move while the tab was open.</strong> <code>assets/app.js</code> records one price point per market on every poll (<code>detectSwing</code> pushes to <code>state.history</code>), but the market page only redrew its chart on an explicit action &mdash; a window button, a ticker change, a resize, or a live-mode status change. So the poller collected points behind a chart that stayed still, which is the one thing a trader watching a sharp move must not see. Reproduced in a headless DOM against a stubbed API that returns a rising price: the history grew from 1 to 3 points over 12 s while no redraw followed. <strong>Fixed:</strong> while the market page is open, a 5-second timer redraws the chart and the order-book ladder from the latest poll (guarded so a redraw error can never take the page down, and it does not re-render tables, so it cannot fight the reader&rsquo;s scrolling). The chart is still built only from published quotes &mdash; no interpolated or invented points.</td><td><code>assets/app.js</code>, <code>market.html</code></td><td><a href="market.html?ticker=KXPRESNOMR-28-MPEN">market page (live)</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-market">Get Market (fields used)</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-ef2c1184.md">verification record</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-67"></span>F-67</td><td>Defect (found in this review, fixed)</td><td><strong>The two alert chats only updated at page load.</strong> <code>alerts-page.js</code> read <code>data/live/feed.json</code> once, in its boot function. The recurring loop polled the demo API from the visitor&rsquo;s browser and re-rendered, but the engine&rsquo;s alerts, its history and its resolution list were never re-read. A trader who left the console open would not see a new &ge;&nbsp;$0.10 swing, or a resolution, until reloading &mdash; which contradicts the &ldquo;live alerting&rdquo; and &ldquo;real time&rdquo; requirement. Reproduced in a headless DOM with a stubbed fetch that returns one extra alert on its second read of the feed: <code>feed reads after boot: 1, new alert visible: false</code>; with the fix, <code>feed reads after 61 s: 2, new alert visible: true</code>. <strong>Fixed:</strong> the page re-reads the feed every 60 seconds (the engine samples every 10 minutes) and re-renders, keeping the scroll position and highlighting only rows that were not on screen before. The header badge still states the measured age of that sample, so &ldquo;the page refreshed&rdquo; is never confused with &ldquo;the exchange just moved&rdquo;.</td><td><code>alerts/js/alerts-page.js</code>, alerts console</td><td><a href="alerts/index.html">Live alerts console</a> · <a href="data/live/feed.json">engine feed</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-ef2c1184.md">verification record (§7)</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-68"></span>F-68</td><td>Over-claim (corrected)</td><td><strong>The executive summary promised more than the engine did.</strong> It stated that every market is followed &ldquo;from <code>active</code> through <code>determined</code> to <code>finalized</code>&rdquo;. Measured against the engine (F-70) a followed market could be evicted before its result was published, and the published feed contained no resolution and no place where an unanswered follow was visible &mdash; so the sentence described an intention, not the product. <strong>Corrected:</strong> the summary now says exactly what is tracked, links the new <a href="alerts/index.html#await-table">Followed to resolution</a> table, and names F-70. A separate limitation was added: Kalshi&rsquo;s WebSocket handshake is authenticated, so a static site cannot stream ticks &mdash; the badge always shows the measured age of the newest sample.</td><td><code>templates/executive-summary.tpl</code>, <code>executive-summary.html</code></td><td><a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/quick_start_websockets">Quick Start: WebSockets (authenticated handshake)</a> · <a href="executive-summary.html">executive summary</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-ef2c1184.md">verification record (§7)</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Corrected</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-70"></span>F-70</td><td>Defect (found in this review, fixed)</td><td><strong>Resolution tracking contradicted the brief in two measured ways, and the shipped feed proved it: zero resolutions recorded and 500 settled markets skipped as &ldquo;untracked&rdquo; in the 2026-11-21 17:42:16Z sample.</strong> (1) <em>Retention.</em> The engine kept a 300-row market map and filled it by estimated traded dollars. A tracked market that fell out of the top 300 before the exchange published its result had that result discarded as untracked — the opposite of <q>track live events until the events have resolved</q>. Reproduced on the committed engine: a market alerted on in a 401-row sample was absent from the map, and a later <code>result: &quot;no&quot;</code> produced <code>resolutions: []</code>. (2) <em>Retrieval.</em> <code>collectResolutions()</code> read a single page of <code>GET /markets?series_ticker=…&status=settled&limit=100</code>. The official Get Markets page states that <code>status=settled</code> alone is <strong>sorted by creation time</strong>, newest first, and that <code>min_settled_ts</code>/<code>max_settled_ts</code> are the filters that sort by <strong>settlement time</strong>, latest first; <code>limit</code> is capped at 1000. So a busy series fills the one page with newly created strikes and a market that resolved this hour is never returned. It also polled the same first 12 series forever instead of only markets still awaiting an answer. <strong>Fixed:</strong> only pending markets are asked about, series whose tracked markets closed most recently are polled first, the request uses <code>min_settled_ts</code> (one 15-minute overlap before the previous sample) at <code>limit=1000</code> and follows the cursor within a two-page budget; alerted markets are never evicted before they resolve (and for 7 days after), <code>settled_markets</code> remembers tracked tickers, and the resolved row keeps <code>result</code>/<code>settlement_ts</code>/<code>settlement_value_dollars</code>. The feed now publishes an <code>awaiting</code> list, shown as <q>Followed to resolution</q> on the alerts page, and the market page states each contract&rsquo;s exchange-published answer. <strong>Not claimed:</strong> no resolution has yet been observed flowing through the fixed path on a scheduled run — the demo markets this engine currently follows close between 2026-12-06 and 2029, so the fix is proven by 14 new offline assertions, not by a live settlement. That remains open below.</td><td><code>tools/live_engine.mjs</code> (retention, <code>collectResolutions</code>), <code>data/live/feed.json</code> (<code>awaiting</code>), <code>alerts/</code>, <code>templates/market.tpl</code>, <code>scripts/check_alerts.mjs</code></td><td><a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-markets.md">Get Markets: sort order, <code>min_settled_ts</code>, <code>limit</code> ≤ 1000</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/market_lifecycle">Market Lifecycle (<code>result</code> is yes/no/scalar)</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets?series_ticker=KXBTCD&amp;status=settled&amp;limit=2">live settled probe (result <code>no</code>, <code>settlement_ts</code> present)</a> · <a href="data/live/feed.json">current feed</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-ef2c1184.md">verification record</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-65"></span>F-65</td><td>Irregularity (upstream API behaviour, measured)</td><td><strong>An unsupported query parameter is silently ignored instead of rejected</strong>, which makes a specific-looking source link return the whole exchange. Measured 2026-11-21 06:40 UTC: <code>GET /trade-api/v2/markets?ticker=KXAAAGASW-26NOV23-3.6400</code> returned the unfiltered market list (28 cursor pages, beginning with unrelated <code>KXMVECROSSCATEGORY</code> markets), while <code>GET /trade-api/v2/markets?tickers=KXAAAGASW-26NOV23-3.6400</code> returned exactly that one market. The official parameter is <code>tickers</code> (plural); no singular <code>ticker</code> is documented for Get Markets. The same singular name <em>is</em> documented for Get Trades, where it filters correctly (checked live: <code>/markets/trades?ticker=KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO</code> returned only that market’s trades). Consequence for verification: a row whose source link used <code>markets?ticker=</code> could not be checked at all. No published row on this site does — <code>scripts/check-row-sources.mjs</code> now fails the build if one ever appears.</td><td>Every row on this site; source links</td><td><a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-markets.md">Get Markets (parameters: tickers)</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-trades.md">Get Trades (parameter: ticker)</a> · <a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets?tickers=KXAAAGASW-26NOV23-3.6400">the working plural probe (live)</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Needs review</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><span id="F-66"></span>F-66</td><td>Limitation (upstream CORS policy, measured)</td><td><strong>A browser cannot send Kalshi’s signed requests, so a static page can never read a portfolio or place an order.</strong> Measured on a GitHub runner twice (2026-11-21T20:20Z, 20:21Z): the preflight a browser must send before a request carrying custom headers is answered <code>204</code> with <code>access-control-allow-methods: GET,POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE,HEAD,OPTIONS</code> — but its <code>access-control-allow-headers</code> list is <code>Origin,Content-Length,Content-Type,Idempotency-Key,X-Csrf-Token,X-Aws-Waf-Token,Traceparent,Tracestate,…</code> and does <strong>not</strong> include <code>KALSHI-ACCESS-KEY</code>, <code>KALSHI-ACCESS-SIGNATURE</code> or <code>KALSHI-ACCESS-TIMESTAMP</code>, which are the three headers Kalshi requires on every authenticated request. A browser therefore fails the preflight and never sends the request, whatever JavaScript does. <strong>Consequence:</strong> the site must not claim that it signs requests, reads an account, or executes trades from your browser. Execution in this repository happens in GitHub Actions, where <code>tools/demo_trader.mjs</code> signs with repository secrets that never reach the page, and the browser only reads the resulting <a href="data/account.json">data/account.json</a>. Copy that claimed in-browser signing (this page’s earlier text, <code>index.html</code> and <code>executive-summary.html</code>) was corrected in the same commit. <strong>Note on the same measurement:</strong> the demo host echoes <em>any</em> requesting origin (<code>http://localhost:8000</code> was also reflected) rather than refusing unknown origins. That is what the exchange does; it is recorded here, not endorsed — and it does not widen what this site can do, because the signature headers are still not allowed.</td><td>Demo-account page; execution claims; browser connectors</td><td><a href="data/cors-probe.json">Raw preflight + GET headers</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-c85ee474.md">Method and both runs</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/quick_start_authenticated_requests.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi: required auth headers</a> · <a href="https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#http-cors-protocol">WHATWG Fetch: preflight</a></td><td><span class="badge warn">Documented limitation</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><span id="F-71"></span>F-71</td><td>Defect (model ranked untradeable swings; fixed)</td><td><strong>The high-ROI chat pane was topped by price swings that could not be executed at the price shown.</strong> Measured on the committed feed of 2026-11-21T17:42:16Z: it held 38 alert rows and 6 carried <code>model.highROI: true</code>. On <strong>5 of those 6</strong> the published quote at detection sat at the opposite end of the book from the swing price — for example <code>KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500</code> at 2026-11-21T17:40:37Z read &ldquo;$0.01 → $0.97 (up)&rdquo; with <code>yes_bid_dollars 0.0000</code> and <code>yes_ask_dollars 0.0100</code>, i.e. nobody could have bought at $0.97; and <code>KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO</code> at 2026-11-20T18:49:04Z read &ldquo;$0.803 → $0.003 (down)&rdquo; with no YES bid at all, so nobody could have sold at $0.003. Two model terms caused it: the spread term scored a <em>one-sided</em> book as &ldquo;tight&rdquo; ($0.97 − $0.00), and nothing checked whether the post-swing quote corroborated the swing price. <strong>Still reproducing on current data:</strong> the sample generated 2026-11-21T20:42:25Z contains the same shape on a <em>different</em> ticker — <code>KXPRESPERSON-28-VRAM</code> &ldquo;$0.802 &rarr; $0.003 (down)&rdquo; with <code>yes_ask 0.8020</code>, <code>yes_bid 0.0000</code>, labelled high ROI on 3 of 12 rows — so this was an ongoing model defect, not one stale file. <strong>Fix (this commit):</strong> a follow-through gate requires the YES ask (on an up move) or the YES bid (on a down move) to sit within $0.02 of the recorded swing price; a side priced at $0.00 means there is nothing to buy from or sell into; untradeable rows are capped at 0.30 and say why in plain words. <code>scripts/check_alerts.mjs</code> now fails a sample in which a high-ROI row is not executable. The 5 historical rows were re-scored in place (0.779/0.787/0.6288 → 0.30, not high ROI); the original v1 score and label are preserved as <code>model.original_score</code>/<code>original_highROI</code> and listed in <code>meta.legacy_unfillable_high_roi</code>. No price, arrow, score history or timestamp was altered, and no row was deleted. The pre-fix formula, reproduced verbatim, still labels both phantom shapes high-ROI, so the new assertions are real guards.</td><td>Live alerts · high-ROI chat · model</td><td><a href="data/live/feed.json">Committed feed + legacy list</a> · <a href="https://demo-api.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official market row (live)</a> · <a href="https://demo-api.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500/orderbook" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official orderbook (live)</a> · <a href="https://demo-api.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets/trades?ticker=KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500&amp;limit=8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official trades (live)</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-trades.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi: Get Trades</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-market.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi: Get Market fields</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/orderbook_responses.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi: only bids are published, and a NO bid is a YES ask at 1 − bid</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-e7260519.md">Measurement and tests</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><span id="F-72"></span>F-72</td><td>Defect (a frozen copy could look live; fixed)</td><td>The header badge ages the alert sample <em>in the visitor&rsquo;s browser</em>. That is correct for a live tab and useless everywhere else: in a screenshot, a printed page, a PDF or an archived copy the words &ldquo;engine 3 min ago&rdquo; stay frozen while the data behind them ages. At claim time this repository&rsquo;s committed feed was about three hours old and the page still rendered a healthy header. <strong>Fix (this commit):</strong> every page (and the standalone alerts console, and the generated category pages) now carries a visible <code>#feed-asof</code> line stating the absolute sample time and the project&rsquo;s own 20-minute &ldquo;late&rdquo; / 45-minute STALE rule, rendered into the HTML at build time so it survives JavaScript being off; <code>assets/freshness.js</code> then replaces it with the age measured at load and re-measures every 30 s. <code>scripts/check_freshness_badge.mjs</code> asserts the line exists on all four tested pages and that a 5-hour-old sample prints STALE. The build-time age word was removed on purpose: a value computed from <code>Date.now()</code> made <code>build-site.mjs --check</code> fail on the next CI run although nothing had changed.</td><td>Every page header</td><td><a href="assets/freshness.js">assets/freshness.js</a> · <a href="scripts/check_freshness_badge.mjs">scripts/check_freshness_badge.mjs</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#schedule">GitHub: a schedule has no cadence guarantee</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td><span id="F-73"></span>F-73</td><td>Defect (contract counts dropped from alerts; fixed going forward, historical rows left blank)</td><td><strong>37 of the 38 published alert rows carried no contract count for either quote</strong> (<code>yes_bid_size_fp</code>/<code>yes_ask_size_fp</code> were absent), even when the sampled market row for that ticker had them. The merge step that attaches an alert to its market row copied the bid, ask, volume and close time but never the two size fields. The brief asks explicitly how many contracts are available at a given price, so this made the chat unable to answer its own question — and it is the same class of error as F-71: a price without a size is not an offer. <strong>Fix (this commit):</strong> the two size fields (and <code>open_interest_fp</code>) are carried onto every alert, asserted by a new selftest case, and the chat now prints the count beside each quote. <strong>Not fixed retroactively:</strong> the historical rows stay blank rather than being backfilled from today&rsquo;s order book, which would state a size that did not exist at the time of the swing. Only 1 of the 38 rows can show a size today, and the page says so instead of guessing.</td><td>Live alerts · chat rows · engine merge step</td><td><a href="data/live/feed.json">Committed feed (count the nulls)</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-market.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi: <code>yes_bid_size_fp</code>/<code>yes_ask_size_fp</code> are published per market</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/orderbook_responses.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi: contract counts per price level</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-e7260519.md">Measurement</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed (historical rows blank)</span></td></tr>
